<!-- STICKY SCROLL — process -->
<section class="case-sticky">
  <div class="case-sticky-text">
    <div class="case-sticky-text-block" data-step="0">
      <div>
        <span class="step">Step 01 · Structure</span>
        <h3>One camp, four journeys.</h3>
        <p>Every camp opens onto a short run of journeys (رحلات), and every journey opens onto a handful of daily missions — read a chapter, answer a reflection question, mark it done. The staircase from "camp" down to "today's task" never has more than three steps in it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="case-sticky-text-block" data-step="1">
      <div>
        <span class="step">Step 02 · Reward system</span>
        <h3>A passport, not a progress bar.</h3>
        <p>Completion is tracked on a passport (جواز السفر) that fills in per journey — a metaphor kids already understand from real travel. Finishing a journey unlocks a badge, finishing a camp unlocks a medal, and the whole camp ends in a certificate they can download and keep.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="case-sticky-text-block" data-step="2">
      <div>
        <span class="step">Step 03 · Tone</span>
        <h3>A camp, not a classroom.</h3>
        <p>Task states read like a scoutmaster, not a teacher's red pen: "Mission accomplished" sits next to "Not done yet, no worries" — never a hard fail, always a next step back into the story.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="case-sticky-text-block" data-step="3">
      <div>
        <span class="step">Step 04 · Community</span>
        <h3>The gathering.</h3>
        <p>الملتقى pulls kids out of solo reading into a shared camp: a live map of where friends are, an evening "samar" session — named after the Arabic tradition of gathering after dark to talk — and a wall to share what they picked up along the way.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
